Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.10004121006510.14867-100000@sor-6.stern.nyu.edu>

>I don't know your data, but I can tell you something about selecting df
>in 
>each smoothed variable by means of AIC, i.e. by using step.gam.
>Sometime the AIC select too many df and so the almost the all variability
>of 
>the response variable is "captured" by the smoothed terms.
>You can see this by increasing or decreasing df in smoothing term: the t 
>value for the other explanotary variables should decrease or increase:
>this 
>is the problem when you use smoothing terms.
>You can try BIC instead, just modify step.gam() or if you know whether
>exist 
>an alternative function in s-plus.

This tendency for AIC to overfit (undersmooth in the nonparametric
function estimation context) is well-known in the model selection
literature. You might want to look at a paper I wrote with Chih-Ling Tsai
about using a modified version of AIC in the semiparametric and additive
model context: "Semiparametric and Additive Model Selection Using an
Improved Akaike Information Criterion," Journal of Computational and
Graphical Statistics, 8, 22-40 (1999). The paper gives a link to S-Plus
functions to use the method, which are available at my home page.

